# Hyperactivity is the dharma; for computer scientists it is a path

Avik Jain

The most concise total understanding of hyperactivity is the dharma; for computer scientists, hyperactivity is a path.

## What hyperactivity is for

Agency and access colocated with identity: malleable local-first software and the collective consciousness of every community at every layer, with the interaction network closing in shared visibility and recognition. Unity on essentials replaces division over superficialities: decentralized education, mutual service at zero marginal cost to the giver, and global permaculture without enclosure. Put directly: freedom from slavery and freedom from delusion — global self-recognition and the healing of the human superorganism.

## There is no identity as a boundary

Communities treated as bounded identities are a pathology — the rise of nations and division, and the exact opposite of hyperactivity. As an enclosing wall around an essence, there is no identity.

Every boundary is drawn by an observer: what counts as a racial category, a nationality, a family, or a religion has been drawn differently in every century, almost always by whoever profited from the line. The count of religions depends entirely on where one cuts; biologists hold more than twenty competing species concepts, and ring species, hybrids, and horizontal gene transfer defeat every one of them; lexicographers who lump and who split disagree on every word because usage is continuous. There is one record and one interaction network; any grouping is a standpoint taken on it — a view rather than a wall, held *in some respect* and never absolutely. The identity colocated with agency and access is the identity of the whole: each person's paths reach every other person.

## Identity is the paths between things: the dharma, in the Jain sense

In univalent foundations identity is not a boundary around an essence: two things are identified by a way of passing from one to the other, and the paths keep extending — paths between paths at every level, nothing closing them off. In Jain ontology *dharma* is the medium of motion, the condition through which souls and matter pass from one place to another (it does not cause motion; it is what passage happens through), and *adharma* is the medium of rest. Identity as paths is dharma in this literal sense. The word derives from the root *dhṛ*, "to hold, to support", cognate with Latin *firmus*, both from Proto-Indo-European *\*dher-*, "to hold firmly": what holds the world together is the ways of passing between things, not boundaries.

## The dharma, in the Buddhist sense

Buddhism uses the word at three levels: the teaching; the way things are, which the teaching points to; and the *dharmas*, the elementary events of which experience consists. Each dharma arises dependent on conditions and has no nature of its own (Nāgārjuna's emptiness: not that nothing exists, but that a thing's being is wholly its relations, with no essence left when they are taken away). In homotopy type theory two types are equal exactly when they are equivalent (Voevodsky's univalence): no hidden essence tells apart two things that correspond perfectly, and a thing is determined wholly by how it relates, through paths, to everything else. That is emptiness. Equality is itself a space of paths, with paths between paths at every level: that is dependent arising, all the way up.

## Computable

In the Homotopy Type Theory book (2013) univalence was an axiom, asserted and not computed: transport along it got stuck. Cohen, Coquand, Huber and Mörtberg's cubical type theory (2015) made paths functions from an interval obeying a De Morgan algebra (the four-cornered structure of is, is not, both, neither) and univalence a theorem with computational content through Glue types; transport along an equivalence runs, and Huber proved canonicity, closed natural numbers computing to numerals. Identity is then not asserted from outside: it is a process the system carries out. To say two things are the same is to have a way of passing between them, and passing along it is a computation.

## Metacircular

McCarthy's `eval` (1960) gave Lisp's meaning in Lisp; Reynolds (1972) and Smith's 3-Lisp (1982) showed what a system holding its own interpreter can do: reason about itself and change itself without leaving itself. Hyperactivity's core holds its own syntax as data (`lib/code.cub`: variables, λ, Π, Σ, paths, interval and face operations), and its checker reads codes back as terms and checks them (`deriving` runs a function of the language that produces code, which the core checks).

## Closure

Equality computes, so the system's sameness is inside it as transport it can run; the system holds its own codes and checker, so its account of what is well formed and what is the same is a value of the system. Nothing the system needs to say what anything is lies outside it. Dignāga's school held that every cognition is aware of itself as it occurs, with no second observer behind it; Dzogchen says the mind's nature is awareness knowing itself, already open and awake, liberated by recognition and not by construction. A closed, metacircular system with computable identity is that structure in mathematics: its knowing of itself is one of its own processes, and identity is passing within it.

Hyperactivity translates the dharma into computing: emptiness, dependent arising, and self-knowing awareness with no outside observer are the properties of a closed, univalent, computable system that describes itself. For a computer scientist, learning it is walking that path.

## What follows

- **The Jain and Buddhist resolution of conflict.** Everyone walking the path, aware of their own ego, meets apparent conflict by seeking mutual understanding rather than victory; the only measure is movement, and universally there is no conflict (as Dzogchen makes explicit).
- **Global permaculture.** Humans living as one with the earth, food grown the way the living system grows itself, with no enclosure of seed or soil.
- **Tesla's world system.** Power and intelligence everywhere, unmetered.
- **Hyperactive education.** Mathematics, physics, chemistry, biology, anticolonial history, and languages taught as one, because reality has no departments; learning is determined by the intelligence and fluency of the educator, and a mind offloaded into a shared computational commons serves whoever walks to it from where they stand.
- **Universal realization.** Every participant awake: the whole recognizing itself.
